   <indicator aggregation-status="1" ascending="0" measure="2">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improvement in the capacity of local government officials to utilize RBM and RBB principles</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">This indicator measures the extent to which the capacity of local government officials (khokimiyats, kengashes, and makhallas) has been strengthened to apply Results-Based Management (RBM) and Results-Based Budgeting (RBB) principles in their planning, budgeting, and public administration processes. It assesses the progress from baseline capacity (pre-training assessment) to improved competency levels (post-training assessment) after capacity-building interventions under the Joint Programme.
Baseline (2023):
Average score of 30% in pre-training assessments.
Target:
40% average score in post-training assessments by 2025;
80% average score in post-training assessments by 2028.
Means of Verification:
Training assessment reports;
Participant feedback surveys;
Independent performance evaluations;
Certification program completion records.
Rationale:
Strengthening the technical and institutional capacities of local government officials is critical to institutionalizing RBM and RBB practices, ensuring better strategic planning, resource management, accountability, and service delivery. It supports the broader goal of establishing coherent, inclusive, and results-driven local governance systems in Uzbekistan.
Key Activities Contributing to the Indicator:
Conducting institutional analysis, functional review, and business process reengineering of local governance bodies;
Developing and delivering customized RBM and RBB training and certification programs;
Supporting the application of RBM and RBB in developing local development strategies and budget programs;
Monitoring and evaluating officials&apos; competencies through standardized pre- and post-training assessments.
Key Assumptions:
Sufficient political commitment and allocation of human and financial resources for capacity development.
Active participation and engagement of local officials in the training and certification programs.
Risk Factors:
Potential turnover of trained local government staff.
Insufficient follow-up support or institutional resistance to adopting RBM/RBB principles.</narrative>
    </description>
    <baseline value="30" year="2024"></baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2028-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2028-12-31"></period-end>
     <target value="80"></target>
     <actual value="30"></actual>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
